WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.020
Speaker 1: Another item the Democrats are
trying to get done here falls on the shoulder,

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:09.380
the shoulders, the mighty shoulders of Elizabeth
Warren.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:15.190
Another New York Times article, Elizabeth
Warren will propose a minimum tax on the nation's

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:16.510
richest companies.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:22.640
Now, this comes on the heels of reporting
from several years ago where there were over

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:30.050
90 companies of the largest corporations in
America that paid zero income tax.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:37.830
Zero, John Deere zero, Amazon zero.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:42.230
This falls this comes on the heels of the
reporting from just a few months ago from

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:52.650
ProPublica of all of the billionaires who
didn't pay any tax, Elon Musk, Jeff Bezos,

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:59.180
Warren Buffett, perennial liberal advocate
for for raising taxes on the wealthy.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.510
He didn't pay very much.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:07.979
Reading from this article, Senator Elizabeth
Warren of Massachusetts and her allies will

00:01:07.979 --> 00:01:13.979
propose a minimum tax on the profits of the
nation's richest companies regardless of what

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:16.869
they say they owe the government.

00:01:16.869 --> 00:01:20.799
As part of the Democrats, three point five
trillion dollar economic and social policy

00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:21.799
package.

00:01:21.799 --> 00:01:27.929
Miss Warren's so-called real corporate profits
tax was a key part of her presidential campaign,

00:01:27.929 --> 00:01:34.600
and she has enlisted Senator Angus King, the
Maine Independent, to help press her case

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:39.700
that profitable companies should be taxed
regardless of loopholes and maneuvers that

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.929
have allowed many of them to avoid federal
corporate income taxes altogether.

00:01:44.929 --> 00:01:50.580
What does it say about our system, the morality
of our system?

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:58.539
That we demonize those who are getting ready
to be evicted from their houses, we demonize

00:01:58.539 --> 00:02:01.060
the housing.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:10.810
And we lift up as paragons as examples of
how to live billionaires who who enjoy outrageous

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:20.010
wealth, unimaginable wealth, who pay nothing
into the system, instead, they live off loans

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:25.860
using stock as collateral, and then that loan
ends up being a liability that they actually

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.319
can get a tax break from.

00:02:29.319 --> 00:02:33.590
It is immoral.

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:35.790
I'm not necessarily opposed.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:48.610
Two billionaires, I am opposed to a system
that allows them to to exist with impunity.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:53.980
Continuing on here, the measure would require
the most profitable companies to pay a seven

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:59.870
percent tax on the earnings they report to
investors known as their annual book value

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:02.540
above 100 million.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:07.680
By taxing the earnings reported to investors,
not to the Internal Revenue Service, Democrats

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:13.140
would be hitting earnings that companies like
to maximize, not the earnings they try hard

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:16.270
to diminish for tax purposes.

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:22.180
And this is what Elizabeth Warren had to say
about this during the presidential campaign.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:28.150
Joe Biden and I disagreed on some tax policies,
but there was one thing we strongly agreed

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:29.840
on.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:36.280
Corporations shouldn't be able to tell shareholders
they were making huge profits, then tell the

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:41.250
IRS they made nothing in profits.

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:43.870
Truer words.

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:49.659
When Jeff Bezos and his cadre of billionaire
pals.

00:03:49.659 --> 00:03:55.049
Benefit from a system that we have that has
made them what they are.

00:03:55.049 --> 00:03:57.590
Of course, there was hard work that went into
it.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:04.920
I'm not saying that doesn't play a role, but
luck plays and plays an outsized role in the

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.959
success of people like this.

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:09.010
It's not just their ingenuity.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:12.890
There are a million points of data.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:21.310
That lead to their success, what's what's
the phrase about preparation and lock when

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:26.520
when combined with some nonsense saying.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:30.539
Fortune favors the well prepared or whatever.

00:04:30.539 --> 00:04:37.740
All of that is true, but without luck and
without the sweat and the and the the tears

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:42.919
and the blood and the sacrifice of people
who work for these corporations, they would

00:04:42.919 --> 00:04:46.310
have nothing.

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:53.139
And to to enjoy the spoils of everything that
is benefiting them that this country offers

00:04:53.139 --> 00:04:56.389
and then to pay zero in tax.

00:04:56.389 --> 00:05:03.150
Whether you be a corporation or whether you
be an individual like Jeff Bezos or Elon Musk,

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:09.180
it is immoral, it is reprehensible, and it
needs to be legislated.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:15.500
Need people on Capitol Hill, elected representatives,
that is, after all, what they do, they represent

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:19.199
us and they need to do more for us.

00:05:19.199 --> 00:05:26.340
And not the people who make major payments
to their PACs, major contributions to their

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:30.099
political campaigns, that is what's most important.

00:05:30.099 --> 00:05:32.870
We, the people.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:34.350
Not we, the corporation

